===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[subversive]] person or [[device]] placed within the [[ranks]] of the [[enemy]].
# {{lb|en|computer security}} A [[malicious]] program that is [[disguise]]d as legitimate software.
#: {{syn|en|Trojan}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Katie Hafner|author2=[[w:John Markoff|John Markoff]]|title=Cyberpunk: Outlaws and Hackers on the Computer Frontier|edition=revised|pages=255-256|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=0684818620
|passage=Worse than what could be observed about the program was the fear that it might be a '''Trojan horse''' program -- apparently innocent, but carrying a string of code instructing the computer to carry out a specific damaging instruction at some later time.}}
# {{lb|en|business}} A [[seeming]]ly [[favorable]] [[offer]] designed to trick customers into making [[exorbitant]] payments.
# {{lb|en|politics}} A person, organization, social movement, piece of [[legislation]], or [[ideology]] with a negative agenda or evil intentions under the [[guise]] of positive values or good intentions.
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=American Bar Association Journal|quotee={{w|John Foster Dulles}}|year=1946|month=October|volume=32|issue=10|page=681|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_aba-journal_1946-10_32_10/page/681/mode/1up|passage=Russia thinks of an atomic authority as a '''Trojan horse''' to get behind the iron curtain and impair her defense, and there is some reason historically for this view.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Peter Saunders|title=Capitalism|year=1995|publisher=University of Minnesota Press|location=Minneapolis|page=38|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/capitalism0000saun/page/38/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8166-2798-1|passage=Seen like this, huge western companies function like a '''Trojan horse''' when they are invited inside the boundaries of LDCs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Efraim Karsh|title=Arafat's War|year=2003|publisher=Grove Press|location=New York|page=64|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/arafatswarmanhis0000kars/page/64/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8021-1758-8|passage=Had the Israelis and the Americans realized that the Oslo accords were merely a “'''Trojan horse'''” designed to promote the higher goal of Palestine’s complete liberation, he argued, “they would never have opened their fortified gates and let it inside their walls.”}}
#* '''2003''', [[w:Paul Krugman|Krugman, Paul R.]], ''The Great Unraveling: Losing Our Way in the New Century'' (2004 reprint), W. W. Norton & Company, {{ISBN|0393326055}}, p. 449:
#*: Indeed, it may turn out to be a '''Trojan horse''' that finally allows conservative ideologues, who have unsuccessfully laid siege to Medicare since the days of Barry Goldwater, to breach its political defenses.

